{
  "id": "COMPOUND:16584 - PROTEIN:5011",
  "verbalization": "Methyl-4,5-Dibromocarboxylic Acid is implicated in the biological process through a series of molecular interactions that ultimately lead to the targeting of HCC1937. The compound is directly linked to CMP-N-acetylneuraminate-beta-1,4-galactoside alpha-2,3-sialyltransferase, an enzyme involved in the synthesis of sialic acid. This enzyme is a target of multiple complex molecules, including (1R,8S,9R,16R)-8,16-bis(4-hydroxyphenyl)-9-[(1R,8S,9R,16R)-4,6,12-trihydroxy-8,16-bis(4-hydroxyphenyl)-15-oxatetracyclo[8.6.1.02,7.014,17]heptadeca-2(7),3,5,10(17),11,13-hexaen-9-yl]-15-oxatetracyclo[8.6.1.02,7.014,17]heptadeca-2(7),3,5,10(17),11,13-hexaene-4,6,12-triol and 6'-O-Z-Para-Methoxycinnamoylharpagide. These molecules, in turn, are targeted by HCC1937, suggesting a cascade of molecular interactions that ultimately result in the compound's effect on the cell line HCC1937. The evidence suggests that Methyl-4,5-Dibromocarboxylic Acid may act through a pathway involving the sialyltransferase enzyme, which is a key player in the synthesis of sialic acid, and that this pathway is modulated by other complex molecules that are themselves targeted by HCC1937.",
